`list.push` mutates its receiver and returns it, and no host language has one
expression that does both -- Go's `append` reassigns, JavaScript's `push`
answers the new length, Kotlin's `add` a Boolean. So the compiler makes the
call a statement of its own wherever it was written, and puts the receiver in
the expression position it left.

The three handlers are the three shapes that reaches: assigning the result back
to the receiver, which says nothing the call has not done; assigning it to
something else, which is the call followed by the copy; and reading through it,
which is the call followed by the read. Before HoistMutations the first two
emitted `m.items = m.items = append(...)`, which is not Go, and
`state.items = state.items.push(...)`, which sets a list variable to a number.

Three targets because the wrong answer differs by language and only one of them
was a build failure.

deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `= m\.items = ` -- Go has no assignment expression, so a second `=` here is a file that does not compile
deny out/none/html/index.html `= state\.items\.push\(` -- push answers the new length in JavaScript; assigning it makes the list a number
deny out/kotlin/android/MainScreen.kt `= items\.add\(` -- add answers a Boolean in Kotlin, which is not the list either

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

component main {
    var items list<string> = ["a"]
    var mirror list<string> = []
    var count = 0

    button(text="self", @click { items = items.push("self") })
    button(text="other", @click { mirror = items.push("other") })
    button(text="nested", @click { count = items.push("nested").length() })
    text(value=string(count))
}
